# Declarative Tooltip and Pointer-Target System

Status: planned

## Goal

Replace SoulSearch's parent-aggregated tooltip lookup with a declarative widget attribute and a generic pointer-target dispatcher scoped to each independently rendered root. Each control owns its current tooltip text; the tooltip presentation layer reads that text from the one control selected by normal pointer targeting.

## Locked architectural decisions

- `tooltip` is a mutable text attribute on any `widgets.Widget` descendant. It is not a callback or getter.
- Controls may update `self.tooltip` whenever their state, hovered row, or backing data changes.
- The tooltip agent reads the current attribute on every update and must not retain a stale cached copy.
- Tooltip lookup stops at the selected pointer target. It never falls back to a parent widget's tooltip.
- Pointer-target eligibility is independent of whether a widget currently has tooltip text.
- Layout containers and parent panels are transparent to pointer targeting by default.
- A panel can explicitly opt into being a pointer target when the panel itself owns an interaction or tooltip.
- Windows are opaque pointer barriers by default even when no descendant under the pointer has a tooltip. Controls behind a window cannot become pointer targets within the window's frame.
- Open modal windows inherit normal window blocking behavior while preserving pointer targeting for their visible descendants.
- Tooltip renderer widgets and their descendants are excluded from pointer targeting.
- Pointer targeting is generic UI infrastructure shared with tooltip behavior; the tooltip system does not independently reverse-walk the widget hierarchy.
- Pointer state and tooltip presentation are owned per independently rendered root/host context, not by a process-global registry. Roots include `gui.Screen` instances and overlay widget roots.
- Existing tooltip text, wrapping, placement, and immediate hover behavior remain characterization behavior during this migration.

## Non-goals

- Do not add tooltip-text getter functions, provider callbacks, or parent-level tooltip aggregators.
- Do not implement HTML-style ancestor tooltip inheritance.
- Do not combine this migration with the separate tooltip frame/branding visual cleanup in `project.todo`.
- Do not add delayed display, focus-triggered display, or Escape dismissal unless they are approved as a separate UX change.
- Do not replace semantic description modules such as `attribute_descriptions`; controls should continue using them when updating their own tooltip text.

## Phase 0 - Characterize the current behavior and freeze the contract

- [x] Inventory every tooltip producer, forwarding method, and renderer host in the main SoulSearch screen, stats popover, and stats overlay.
- [x] Record which tooltips are static and which depend on a row, column, picker selection, filter action, or subject record.
- [x] Add or retain characterization tests for current tooltip text, wrapping, placement, and immediate display behavior.
- [x] Retain a characterization test proving that an open picker prevents the underlying filter-management controls from producing tooltips.
- [x] Record the new pointer-contract cases for sibling overlap, transparent panels, blocking windows, excluded tooltip renderers, no parent fallback, hidden ancestors, clipping, and independently rendered roots.
- [x] Keep the new pointer-contract cases as documented acceptance scenarios until the dispatcher test seam is introduced in Phase 2; Phase 0 must not leave the existing suite failing against an abstraction that does not exist yet.
- [x] Record the baseline unit-test count and package/build validation commands in this document before implementation begins.

### Phase 0 inventory and acceptance scenarios

Current renderer hosts and forwarding paths:

- The main `SoulSearchScreen` creates `SoulSearchTooltip` with a callback to `SoulSearchWindow:get_tooltip_text()`.
- `SoulSearchStatsPopoverScreen` creates the same renderer with a callback through its `UnitStatsList`.
- `SoulSearchStatsOverlay` creates the same renderer with a callback through its overlay-hosted `UnitStatsList`.
- `SoulSearchWindow:get_tooltip_text()` is the legacy precedence aggregator. It consults the main filter button and `FilterPanel` controls first, then filter actions, result headers, stats, and filter descriptors.
- `StatsPanel:get_tooltip_text()` only forwards to `UnitStatsList:get_tooltip_text()`.

Current tooltip producers:

- Static: main/filter-management controls in `FILTER_CONTROL_TOOLTIPS`; result-column headers in `ResultsPanel`; stats-column headers and the stats value-cell explanation in `UnitStatsList`.
- Dynamic by filter action: `FilterActionList` action text, including race include/exclude wording.
- Dynamic by picker choice: active, attribute, and race descriptor descriptions; the selected picker and its available rows determine the text. Skill, preset, and unit-scope picker rows currently do not supply descriptor tooltips.
- Dynamic by subject record and row/column: `UnitStatsList` resolves a hovered stats row against the current subject records. `attribute_descriptions` remains the semantic source of descriptor text.

Existing legacy characterization coverage is intentionally retained in `ui_characterization_test.lua`, `filter_panel_test.lua`, `results_panel_test.lua`, and `unit_stats_list_test.lua`. `ui_tooltip_test.lua` covers the renderer's immediate callback read, wrapping, and screen-edge placement. The filter-panel picker test proves that an open picker suppresses underlying filter-action tooltip lookup; it is a legacy behavior test, not an early implementation of the Phase 2 dispatcher.

Phase 2 pointer-contract acceptance scenarios (documentation only until its dispatcher seam exists):

- Overlapping siblings resolve in reverse render order.
- A transparent `Panel` passes through when none of its eligible descendants wins.
- A visible `Window` blocks its whole frame, including its border and empty interior, while allowing descendants to win first.
- The tooltip renderer is `none`: neither it nor any child can become a target.
- A target with no tooltip does not fall back to a parent tooltip.
- A hidden or inactive widget, or one below a hidden/inactive ancestor, is ineligible.
- A target cannot be selected outside its clipped visible bounds.
- Independently rendered screen and overlay roots keep separate pointer contexts and never target across roots.

These scenarios remain documented acceptance criteria in this phase. They will become dispatcher tests in Phase 2; Phase 0 must not add tests against an API that does not yet exist.

### Phase 0 baseline validation

Run from the repository root before migration work:

- `./tools/Test.ps1` — complete pure-Lua suite.
- `./tools/Build.ps1` — production Lua syntax validation.
- `./tools/Publish.ps1` — package validation; this script invokes `VerifyPackage.ps1` with the required package arguments.

Baseline result (2026-07-14): `Test.ps1` passed **262 tests**; `Build.ps1` passed syntax validation for **54 Lua files**. `Publish.ps1` rebuilt the package but its built-in verifier currently fails before archive comparison because `VerifyPackage.ps1` still requires the removed `internal/soulsearch/keybinding_preferences.lua` bootstrap dependency. This pre-existing package-verifier mismatch is recorded here and is outside the declarative-tooltip migration; do not treat it as tooltip implementation failure.

### Phase 0 exit criteria

- [x] Current tooltip behavior is protected by passing characterization tests, and the new pointer-target contract is documented for implementation and testing in Phase 2.

## Phase 1 - Add the common declarative widget attribute

- [x] Audit the active DFHack widget API for an existing `tooltip` attribute or naming collision.
- [x] Evaluate native `widgets.TooltipLabel` and `widgets.DimensionsTooltip`; document which rendering pieces, if any, are reusable and why neither class replaces the required declarative pointer-target system.
- [x] Add one idempotent SoulSearch widget-extension module that declares `tooltip=DEFAULT_NIL` on the common `widgets.Widget.ATTRS` base before SoulSearch widgets are constructed.
- [x] Make the base-attribute extension collision-safe: preserve a compatible DFHack-provided attribute and fail clearly instead of overwriting an incompatible existing contract.
- [x] Verify that native widget classes such as `HotkeyLabel`, `CycleHotkeyLabel`, `Label`, and `List` accept `tooltip='text'` in their normal constructors without custom subclasses.
- [x] Define the attribute contract: a string is current tooltip text; `nil` or an empty string means no tooltip; functions and other value types are invalid.
- [x] Specify that unsupported tooltip value types are validated when the tooltip agent reads the attribute; direct Lua field assignment must not require an observable property setter.
- [x] Verify that assigning `widget.tooltip` after construction updates the value observed on the next tooltip update.
- [x] Load the widget-extension module through every normal independently constructed UI host, including the main screen, stats popover, and stats overlay, before any tooltip-bearing widget instances are constructed.
- [x] Ensure direct component-test imports also load the extension instead of depending on `module_registry.lua` reload ordering.
- [x] Ensure repeated module loads/reloads do not replace DFHack classes, duplicate attributes, or retain stale widget-extension state.
- [x] Update the test environment and widget harness to model the same inherited `tooltip` attribute behavior.
- [x] Add focused tests for constructor assignment, direct runtime mutation, clearing, and reload idempotence.

### Phase 1 API audit and attribute contract

The active DFHack installation's `gui.widgets.Widget.ATTRS` defines `frame`, `frame_inset`, and `frame_background`; it has no `tooltip` attribute. `DEFAULT_NIL` is DFHack's unique nil-default token, so `tooltip=DEFAULT_NIL` is compatible with normal inherited attribute construction and represents an initially absent value.

`widgets.TooltipLabel` is a wrapped label whose `show_tooltip` value only controls its visibility. `widgets.DimensionsTooltip` is a resizing map-selection display that computes a `WxHxZ` label from a map anchor. The latter demonstrates mouse-relative placement, but neither provides generic terminal-widget hit detection, mutable text ownership, modal blocking, or per-root isolation. SoulSearch therefore retains its own renderer during this migration and reuses neither native class.

`internal/soulsearch/ui/widget_extensions.lua` extends only the existing `widgets.Widget.ATTRS` table; it does not replace a DFHack class. On first load it adds `tooltip=DEFAULT_NIL`. On later loads it preserves that exact compatible attribute; any other existing default fails clearly as an incompatible contract. `module_registry.lua` loads the extension before widget modules, and every module that constructs a native widget requests it directly so component imports do not rely on registry ordering. Main-screen, stats-popover, and stats-overlay hosts request it before constructing their roots.

Attribute contract: a string is the current tooltip text; `nil` and `''` mean no tooltip. Functions and all non-string non-nil values are invalid. Direct `widget.tooltip` assignment remains ordinary Lua field mutation with no setter or subscription requirement. Phase 3's tooltip agent will read and validate the current value each update, so a direct mutation is visible on its next sample.

### Phase 1 exit criteria

- [x] Any DFHack widget descendant can declare or update tooltip text without a tooltip-specific subclass or getter.

## Phase 2 - Introduce generic per-root pointer targeting

- [x] Define `PointerContext` as per-root pointer state and `PointerDispatcher` as the service that resolves and updates that state.
- [x] Return an explicit tagged result for `target`, `blocked`, or `miss`, including pointer coordinates local to a selected target.
- [x] Add a common `pointer_policy` contract with `target`, `pass`, `block`, and `none` values.
- [x] Audit the common pointer-policy and pointer-event attribute names for DFHack collisions before extending the shared widget classes.
- [x] Make repeated pointer-attribute extension loads idempotent and preserve compatible DFHack-provided contracts.
- [x] Define policy behavior: descend into eligible children first; `target` selects the widget if no child wins; `pass` falls through; `block` returns blocked; `none` excludes the widget and its complete subtree.
- [x] Make pointer eligibility independent of `tooltip`; adding, changing, or clearing tooltip text must never alter which widget is targeted.
- [x] Default terminal/leaf widgets to `target`.
- [x] Default ordinary `widgets.Panel` layout containers to `pass`, while allowing an individual panel to opt into `target` or `block`.
- [x] Default visible `widgets.Window` instances to `block`, preserving descendant targeting and blocking underlying siblings throughout the window frame when no descendant wins.
- [x] Keep `ModalPanelWindow` on the `block` policy while it is open/visible; a closed/hidden modal does not participate in targeting.
- [x] Add common `on_pointer_enter`, `on_pointer_update`, and `on_pointer_leave` event attributes so native widgets can update their own tooltip state without custom subclasses.
- [x] Define the event signatures as `on_pointer_enter(target, x, y)`, `on_pointer_update(target, x, y)`, and `on_pointer_leave(target)`, where `x` and `y` are local to the terminal target and the callback updates `target.tooltip` when needed.
- [x] Sample pointer state once per root render/update tick, before tooltip presentation, because DFHack does not provide a continuous hovered-widget event stream.
- [x] Emit `on_pointer_leave` and `on_pointer_enter` when the target changes, then emit `on_pointer_update` with current local coordinates for the selected target on every sample.
- [x] Resolve overlapping siblings in reverse render order so the last-rendered eligible widget wins; keyboard focus-owner priority must not override the mouse target.
- [x] Respect evaluated `visible` and `active` state across the complete ancestor chain.
- [x] Respect frame clipping so a widget cannot be targeted outside its visible clipped bounds.
- [x] Clear or replace the current target safely when it closes, becomes hidden, moves, or is removed.
- [x] Distinguish control body hit regions from blocking window-frame regions so window borders also occlude underlying controls.
- [x] Ensure an empty area inside any visible window returns `blocked` instead of exposing a widget behind the window.
- [x] Ensure pointer positions outside a window frame can continue to lower siblings when allowed by the surrounding root.
- [x] Support independently rendered roots for `gui.Screen` instances and `overlay.OverlayWidget` hosts without sharing pointer state.
- [x] Treat the supplied root as a traversal boundary with implicit `pass` behavior; the root itself is never returned as a terminal pointer target regardless of its inherited widget policy.
- [x] Update `soulsearch_env.lua` and `widget_harness.lua` to model pointer policies, tagged results, root-boundary behavior, clipping, and pointer-event callback signatures consistently with production.
- [x] Add focused dispatcher tests for nested controls, transparent panels, explicit panel targets, blocking windows, window borders, sibling overlap, clipping, visibility, target removal, and independently rendered roots.
- [x] Add nested-modal tests proving that the topmost picker blocks the filter window only within the picker's frame, while positions outside the picker can still target the containing filter window.

### Phase 2 implementation notes

The active DFHack Lua API has no `pointer_policy`, `on_pointer_enter`, `on_pointer_update`, or `on_pointer_leave` members. SoulSearch therefore adds those attributes idempotently to the existing widget classes: `Widget` defaults to `target`, `Panel` to `pass`, and `Window` to `block`. The event attributes use `DEFAULT_NIL`, retaining ordinary Lua callback assignment on native widgets. An incompatible pre-existing contract fails instead of being replaced.

`PointerDispatcher.sample(context)` obtains one mouse position from `dfhack.screen.getMousePos()` when coordinates are not supplied by a host. It resolves a supplied root's child tree in reverse render order, treating that root as an implicit `pass` boundary. `frame_body` provides the clip-aware target hit region; `Window` fallback blocking uses its full frame so borders and empty interiors occlude lower siblings. Each context stores only its own target and tagged result, permitting screen and overlay hosts to keep independent state. Host render/update wiring and tooltip presentation remain Phase 3 work.

### Phase 2 exit criteria

- [x] Each independently rendered root can identify exactly one current pointer target, or an explicit miss/blocked result, without tooltip-specific hierarchy traversal.

## Phase 3 - Make tooltip presentation consume pointer state

- [x] Separate the existing tooltip renderer from tooltip discovery and component-specific content lookup.
- [x] Add one tooltip agent/host per independently rendered root context that observes its `PointerContext`.
- [x] Have the agent read only `pointer_context.target.tooltip` after pointer transition handling has completed.
- [x] Read the attribute on every update so direct control mutations are visible without callbacks or change subscriptions.
- [x] Validate the current tooltip value at read time and fail clearly for functions or other unsupported value types instead of invoking or silently accepting them.
- [x] Hide the tooltip immediately when there is no target, the target is blocked, or the current attribute is `nil`/empty.
- [x] Preserve the existing tooltip wrapping, screen-edge positioning, pens, and immediate hover timing.
- [x] Assign the tooltip renderer `pointer_policy='none'` so neither the renderer nor its label/subviews participate in target resolution.
- [x] Render the tooltip above ordinary root content without changing the pointer target selected beneath it.
- [x] Ensure tooltip rendering cannot leak pointer state or text between separate SoulSearch windows, popovers, or overlay instances.
- [x] Add focused tests for live text mutation, clearing text while hovered, target changes, renderer-subtree exclusion, window blocking, and per-root isolation.

### Phase 3 implementation notes

`TooltipAgent` owns one `PointerContext` and one renderer for each root. It samples the mouse once, completes pointer transition handling, reads only the selected terminal target's current `tooltip` field, and passes the resulting text and sampled coordinates to `SoulSearchTooltip`. The renderer no longer accepts a getter callback or reads the mouse itself; it retains the existing formatting, placement, pens, and immediate rendering behavior.

The main screen and stats popover update their agents before their root render pass; the stats overlay updates its agent during its normal overlay update. Each host constructs separate agent, context, and renderer instances. `SoulSearchTooltip` is explicitly `pointer_policy='none'`, so it remains topmost for rendering without becoming a target or obscuring the content below it.

### Phase 3 exit criteria

- [x] Tooltip presentation depends only on its root's current pointer target and that target's current `tooltip` attribute.

## Phase 4 - Migrate static tooltip declarations

- [x] Move static main-window and filter-management button tooltips directly onto their native widget declarations.
- [x] Move results-column header tooltips directly onto the corresponding native `CycleHotkeyLabel` widgets.
- [x] Move stats-column header tooltips directly onto the corresponding header widgets.
- [x] Move picker buttons, close buttons, scope controls, and preset controls with static text to their own `tooltip` attributes.
- [x] Remove `FILTER_CONTROL_TOOLTIPS` and other centralized view-id-to-text tables once all entries are declared by their controls.
- [x] Remove static tooltip hit-test helpers that exist only to support parent aggregation.
- [x] Update component tests to assert the widgets' declared attributes rather than calling parent tooltip lookup methods.
- [x] Add integration coverage proving a native widget's declared static tooltip is found through normal pointer targeting.

### Phase 4 implementation notes

Static strings now live beside the native controls they describe: main and filter-management buttons, searchable/preset-picker close controls, preset save, result `CycleHotkeyLabel` headers, and stats headers. `FILTER_CONTROL_TOOLTIPS`, `HEADER_TOOLTIPS`, `get_control_tooltip()`, `get_button_tooltip()`, and `get_header_tooltip()` have been removed. The remaining `FilterPanel` and `UnitStatsList` tooltip lookup code is dynamic descriptor/action/body behavior reserved for Phase 5.

### Phase 4 exit criteria

- [x] Every static tooltip is declared next to the control it describes, with no parent-maintained tooltip table.

## Phase 5 - Migrate dynamic tooltip ownership

- [x] Update `FilterActionList` to derive and assign its own `tooltip` during `on_pointer_update` and clear it when no action owns the local position.
- [x] Update searchable picker/list target widgets to assign existing descriptor tooltip text for their own hovered choices through `on_pointer_update`.
- [x] Migrate only dynamic descriptions found in the Phase 0 inventory; do not invent new skill, preset, or unit-scope tooltip content as part of the architecture change.
- [x] Keep existing attribute, race, active-filter, and stats description lookup in the terminal control that owns the displayed content.
- [x] Give each `UnitStatsList` header target its static tooltip directly and have its body-label target assign stat, delta, and row-description tooltip text from local pointer coordinates and current subject records.
- [x] Use the common pointer-event attributes when a native child widget needs dynamic behavior; do not create a tooltip-specific subclass solely to receive pointer updates.
- [x] Ensure dynamic controls refresh `tooltip` when backing data changes while the pointer remains over the same target.
- [x] Ensure blank rows, separators, padding, and non-descriptive regions explicitly clear dynamic tooltip text.
- [x] Remove tooltip forwarding methods from `StatsPanel`, picker parents, and other composite controls after their children own the text.
- [x] Add focused component tests for coordinate-to-text mapping and direct attribute updates without involving the tooltip renderer.

### Phase 5 implementation notes

`FilterActionList` now maps terminal-local coordinates to its action zone or active descriptor and updates its own `tooltip`; race include/exclude wording remains the existing special case. Attribute and race picker lists use native `on_pointer_update` callbacks and retain only the prior descriptor text. Skill, preset, and unit-scope rows remain intentionally undescribed.

`UnitStatsList` keeps static header attributes and assigns the body label's text from its local value/label cells and current display records. Every handler runs on each pointer sample, so changed choices or subject records are reflected while the same target remains hovered. Blank rows and non-descriptive regions clear the terminal field. The old parent getters and stats forwarding methods have been removed.

### Phase 5 exit criteria

- [x] Dynamic terminal controls own and maintain their current tooltip text; no ancestor is consulted for tooltip content.

## Phase 6 - Remove legacy aggregation and wire every host

- [x] Wire `SoulSearchScreen` to construct and update one pointer context and tooltip agent for its root widget tree.
- [x] Wire the stats popover screen and stats overlay widget root to their own isolated pointer contexts and tooltip agents.
- [x] Remove `SoulSearchWindow:get_tooltip_text()` and its hard-coded precedence chain.
- [x] Remove `FilterPanel:get_control_tooltip()`, `get_filter_action_tooltip()`, `get_descriptor_tooltip()`, and superseded button helpers.
- [x] Remove `ResultsPanel:get_header_tooltip()`, `UnitStatsList:get_tooltip_text()`, and tooltip-only forwarding methods after their replacements are active.
- [x] Remove `get_text` callback wiring from `SoulSearchTooltip` construction and rename the renderer/host types if their old names no longer describe their responsibilities.
- [x] Remove duplicated visibility and mouse-over helpers that are no longer used outside legacy tooltip discovery.
- [x] Update `module_registry.lua` contracts and reload ordering for the widget extension, pointer infrastructure, and tooltip host while retaining the normal-import guarantees established in Phase 1.
- [x] Search production code and tests for obsolete getter/provider callback APIs and parent-level tooltip precedence logic.
- [x] Replace legacy parent-precedence tests with pointer-target and control-ownership integration tests.

### Phase 6 exit criteria

- [x] No window or parent component aggregates descendant tooltips, and no tooltip renderer accepts a content getter callback.

## Phase 7 - Validate reloads, packaging, and live modal behavior

- [ ] Author and maintain focused pointer, modal-panel, filter-panel, results-panel, stats-panel, popover, overlay, and tooltip test files; execute them through the repository's full-suite runner, which currently loads every registered suite.
- [ ] Run the complete test suite with `tools/Test.ps1` and record the final passing count.
- [ ] Run `tools/Build.ps1` for production Lua syntax validation.
- [ ] Run `tools/Publish.ps1`; use its built-in `VerifyPackage.ps1` invocation instead of calling `VerifyPackage.ps1` without its mandatory package arguments.
- [ ] Reload SoulSearch repeatedly in a live DFHack session and verify the common widget attribute remains available without duplicate-class or stale-module failures.
- [ ] Verify static tooltips on native widgets in the main SoulSearch window.
- [ ] Verify dynamic filter-action, picker-choice, results-header, stats-header, and stats-row tooltips.
- [ ] Open every filter picker and confirm underlying filter-management tooltips never appear through the modal.
- [ ] Move the pointer across picker padding and blank rows and confirm stale tooltip text is cleared.
- [ ] Open multiple SoulSearch windows and confirm pointer targets and tooltip text remain isolated per window.
- [ ] Verify the stats popover and overlay use the same declarative behavior without sharing state with the main screen.
- [ ] Update this plan, relevant architecture documentation, and the `project.todo` tracking entry with final completion status.

### Phase 7 exit criteria

- [ ] Unit tests, build/package validation, reload checks, and live modal/input scenarios all pass.

## Final acceptance criteria

- [ ] Any native or SoulSearch widget can declare `tooltip='text'` without a custom tooltip subclass.
- [ ] Controls can dynamically replace or clear their tooltip attribute and the visible tooltip reflects the new value on the next update.
- [ ] Tooltip content is never supplied through a getter/provider callback.
- [ ] Tooltip lookup never falls back to a parent.
- [ ] Pointer-target eligibility never depends on the current tooltip value.
- [ ] Parent/layout panels are transparent unless explicitly assigned another pointer policy.
- [ ] Visible windows block all underlying controls within their frame unless explicitly assigned another pointer policy.
- [ ] Open modal windows preserve descendant targeting and block underlying controls within their frame.
- [ ] The tooltip renderer and its complete subtree are excluded from pointer targeting.
- [ ] Tooltip resolution, text, and visibility state are isolated per independently rendered root instance.
- [ ] No parent window contains a descendant-tooltip map or precedence chain.
